    "24": {
      "label": "As the Solar System orbits the Milky Way at ~220 km/s, it encounters a \"wind\" of dark matter particles from the galactic halo. The Sun's gravity deflects these particles (assumed non-interacting except gravitationally), focusing them downstream—like a lens concentrating light—creating a region of slightly higher dark matter density behind the Sun relative to the direction of motion.This gravitational focusing enhances the local dark matter phase-space distribution, forming a subtle \"wake\" or directional stream. As Earth orbits the Sun, it periodically moves into and out of this focused region, inducing a small annual variation in the expected dark matter interaction rate at detectors.A landmark 2014 study (Lee et al., Physical Review Letters) showed this effect shifts the phase of the annual modulation signal from the naive expectation (peak June 1) by days to weeks, depending on particle mass and velocity—most pronounced for lower-mass (10 GeV) or slower dark matter. The modulation am\nkonstructivizm\nX\n · \n1d",
